  "id": "Compound::DB00468 - Gene::6584",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence strongly suggests a relationship between Quinine and Slc22a5, mediated through Quinidine, a closely related alkaloid. Quinine and Quinidine share a common ancestor in the Cinchona Alkaloid class and are both classified as antimalarial agents. The evidence indicates that Quinine causes various symptoms, including Angina Symptom, Blindness, and Blackwater Fever, all of which are linked to Quinidine. Quinidine binds to Slc22a5, a protein involved in ion transport, suggesting a potential mechanism by which Quinidine exerts its effects on cellular function. The recurring pattern of Quinine causing symptoms and Quinidine binding to Slc22a5 implies a broader pathway where Quinine may influence Slc22a5 through its interaction with Quinidine. This connection is further supported by the shared ontological lineage of Quinine and Quinidine within the Cinchona Alkaloid class, reinforcing the likelihood of a functional relationship between the two entities.",
